 ## Background
-The use of octrees for volume rendering originated as a solution to the memory and computational inefficiencies of uniform grids for sparse data. Early work focused on branch-on-need octrees to store sparse data compactly. The integration of ray traversal algorithms with these structures allowed for efficient empty-space skipping, which became a standard technique in high-performance volume rendering. The development of GPU architectures enabled the implementation of stackless and mini-stack traversal methods, optimizing memory access patterns for parallel execution. More recent advancements include the use of rational arithmetic for robustness [S1], rasterization-based culling for performance [S2, unofficial], and JIT specialization for dynamic data types [S1].
+The concept of using hierarchical structures for volume rendering dates back to the early 1990s, with foundational work on Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) enabling efficient storage and traversal of large volumetric datasets. The specific algorithmic framework for ray traversal within these trees, often referred to as the "Amanatides-Woo" algorithm adapted for trees, was popularized in the context of real-time rendering and global illumination. Key developments include the introduction of stackless traversal techniques to optimize GPU performance [S1], and the use of neighbor pointers to accelerate empty-space skipping [S2, unofficial]. The method is widely used in modern real-time ray tracers, global illumination techniques like voxel cone tracing [S1], and interactive visualization of time-varying data [S2, unofficial].
 
